WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.710
A scam filter caught ninety-nine

2
00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.950
percent of fake messages in testing.

3
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.820
Then scammers changed a few words, and

4
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.400
almost everything got through.

5
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.800
Nobody had tested it against

6
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.480
someone trying to beat it.

7
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.630
Today: three ways attackers go after a

8
00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.950
model itself, and what each defence costs.

9
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.100
The first is evasion, with an

10
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.360
adversarial example.

11
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.420
A small, chosen change to the

12
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.940
input flips the answer, while a

13
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.300
person sees no difference.

14
00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.730
A few pixels, a sticker on a road

15
00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.330
sign, a few letters in a message.

16
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.350
How is the nudge found?

17
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.340
For a linear model, the score is the

18
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.900
weights times the inputs, plus a bias.

19
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.920
The fastest way down moves each input a

20
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.680
small step against its weight's sign.

21
00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.540
That's the fast gradient sign idea,

22
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.060
and deep networks fall to it too.

23
00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.950
In Python, it's one line per input.

24
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.930
Each input steps by epsilon

25
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.350
against its weight's sign.

26
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.640
The score then drops by epsilon times

27
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.280
the sum of the weights' sizes: that's

28
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.100
how far a defender must test.

29
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:18.800
Quick check!

30
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.780
A model is ninety-nine percent

31
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.080
sure a picture shows a cat.

32
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.650
Can a tiny change still flip its answer?

33
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:29.180
Yes.

34
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.710
Confidence tells you how sure the model

35
00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.570
is, not how far the input is from

36
00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:35.850
flipping.

37
00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.450
In the lab, about eight pixels

38
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.210
usually flip a confident answer.

39
00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:41.770
Sure isn't safe.

40
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.280
The second attack is extraction: thousands

41
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.840
of queries, and a copy trained on the

42
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.180
answers, taking what cost you months.

43
00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.370
The third is membership inference:

44
00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.070
guessing whether a person's data was

45
00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.470
in the training set, from how sure

46
00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:00.690
the model is about them.

47
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.580
Every defence has a cost.

48
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.860
Adversarial training makes attacks harder,

49
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.660
but can lower accuracy on normal inputs.

50
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.000
Privacy noise blocks membership guesses,

51
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.200
but makes the model less useful.

52
00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.190
Rate limits, and returning labels

53
00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.610
instead of exact scores, slow

54
00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.590
extraction but annoy honest users.

55
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.500
So match each defence to its

56
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.580
threat, and measure both sides.

57
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.680
Test accuracy on clean inputs and on

58
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.620
nudged ones, with the same budget.

59
00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.710
And where it matters, never let one

60
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.210
model's confident answer decide alone.

61
00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.170
In the app, train a small shapes model,

62
00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:48.910
set the nudge strength to one, then raise

63
00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:51.390
the pixels allowed until a drawing flips.

64
00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.790
Press “Train it tough” and attack again.

65
00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.120
At ten pixels, attacks that fool it

66
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.120
fall from about eighty-two percent

67
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.040
to about forty-one percent.

68
00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:03.770
Your turn.

69
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.840
Your model's API returns exact confidence

70
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.500
scores to anyone, with no limits.

71
00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.680
Which attacks does that make easiest?

72
00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.590
Extraction and membership inference both

73
00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:20.350
feed on exact scores and unlimited

74
00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:20.810
queries.

75
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.560
Remember: confidence isn't robustness,

76
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.700
test with the nudge against the weights,

77
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.200
scores feed privacy attacks, and measure

78
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.700
what every defence costs.

79
00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.820
Try it tomorrow: take a simple model

80
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.780
you've built, or the lab's.

81
00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.740
Test it on clean inputs and on slightly

82
00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.180
nudged ones, and write down both

83
00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:44.920
accuracies.

84
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.760
Then add one defence, and

85
00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.500
measure its cost.
